{
  "id": "home/Base_Figure",
  "name": "Figure beside prose",
  "plugin": "biom-doc",
  "variables": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "how-to-use-this",
      "html": "<!-- guest/sections/default.html — what a section with no `data:` resolves to.\n\n     ONE CENTRED SLOT NAMED `body`, at a reading measure.\n\n     THIS IS A FILE AND NOT A BRANCH IN THE RUNTIME, and that is load-bearing\n     rather than tidy. A default that lived in the runtime would be the one\n     section shape nothing else could reach: it would need no HTML file, no\n     `data:` key and no slot declaration, and every other section would be the\n     exception to it. \"The document\" would be privileged all over again —\n     exactly what the deleted render layer kept collapsing into, where one\n     render owned the whole sheet and prose was one column at one measure\n     because there was nowhere else for it to be.\n\n     As a file it is the same mechanism every other section uses. It is read by\n     the server, sent inline in the `page.read` answer like any section's markup,\n     and drawn by the same code path. It is legible, it can be copied as the\n     starting point for a real section, and it can be replaced. A page that is\n     mostly defaults is an ordinary document. The runtime still marks one\n     `data-g-default` so the editor can tell a default section from an authored\n     one; no checker rule reads it.\n\n     THE MEASURE LIVES HERE, ON THE SECTION, RATHER THAN ON THE PAGE. That is\n     what makes a full-bleed section cost nothing: it simply does not carry this\n     file's `max-width`, and there is no sheet-level width for it to fight.\n\n     AND THE MEASURE IS FOR PROSE. When the one slot holds a grid — the\n     document's own table, which the doc plugin makes out of every markdown\n     table it finds in a paragraph and puts in a section of its own with no\n     file of its own, which is this one — the section widens to the figure\n     frame's width and the grid takes it, scrolling sideways inside its own box\n     only past that. The runtime says what a slot holds on the slot, as\n     `data-g-kind`, so this file can size by it without knowing what any\n     plugin draws; a section with a file of its own says its own width. -->\n\n<!-- No colour is set here, deliberately. Every colour in this product resolves\n     to a token the palette rewrites at runtime, and a section that reached for\n     one would be the section that stopped theming. Type and colour are inherited\n     from what the shim declares on `:root` when the theme arrives. -->\n<style>\n  /* `:scope` is this section's own element. The runtime wraps everything in this\n     file in `@scope (#sec-<name>)`, so these rules cannot reach the section\n     above or the section below however ordinary the selectors look. */\n  :scope {\n    display: block;\n  }\n\n  .measure {\n    /* The workspace's own reading measure when its `markdown.yaml` names one,\n       and 34rem when it does not. The scale OFFERS the value on the page root\n       and imposes it nowhere: this file is the section that wants it, which is\n       what keeps a full-bleed section free of it. */\n    max-width: var(--md-measure, 34rem);\n    margin-inline: auto;\n    padding-block: 1.5rem;\n    padding-inline: 1.25rem;\n  }\n\n  /* A GRID IS NOT PROSE. The measure opens to the figure frame's width — the\n     same 64rem the doc plugin's `.wrap` gives a figure — and the slot inside\n     it lets go of the reading measure the frame holds every slot to. An\n     unlayered rule here beats the frame's layered one by existing, which is\n     the frame's own bargain. */\n  .measure:has(> [data-g-kind=\"grid\"]) { max-width: 64rem; }\n  [data-g-kind=\"grid\"] { max-inline-size: none; }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"measure\">\n  <div data-g-part=\"body\"></div>\n</div>\n",
      "fallback": true,
      "parts": {
        "body": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "md": "Copy `index.html` beside a page's `content.yaml` — `figure.html` is the obvious name — and\npaste the section below into that page's `contents` with `data:` changed to match. Or copy\nthis whole directory into `pages/<parent>/children/<name>/` and it draws as it stands.\n\nThe picture is held while the prose scrolls past it, and it is released where this section\nends: sticky only pins inside its own containing block. That is why the figure and the\nargument are ONE section rather than two.\n\n**The figure is a drawing and not a photograph.** A row of counts over a row of bars, in\nHTML, on the palette's tokens, growing once when the section is first seen — which is what a\nfigure is here. Its words and its numbers are the section's `variables`, as parallel lists,\nso changing the drawing is changing the document.\n",
          "vars": {}
        }
      },
      "vars": {},
      "source": {
        "name": "how-to-use-this",
        "parts": {
          "body": "Copy `index.html` beside a page's `content.yaml` — `figure.html` is the obvious name — and\npaste the section below into that page's `contents` with `data:` changed to match. Or copy\nthis whole directory into `pages/<parent>/children/<name>/` and it draws as it stands.\n\nThe picture is held while the prose scrolls past it, and it is released where this section\nends: sticky only pins inside its own containing block. That is why the figure and the\nargument are ONE section rather than two.\n\n**The figure is a drawing and not a photograph.** A row of counts over a row of bars, in\nHTML, on the palette's tokens, growing once when the section is first seen — which is what a\nfigure is here. Its words and its numbers are the section's `variables`, as parallel lists,\nso changing the drawing is changing the document.\n"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "spread",
      "html": "<!-- base/figure/index.html — AN INSTRUMENT HELD BESIDE THE PROSE THAT EXPLAINS IT.\n\n     WHAT IT DRAWS. Two slots side by side, and the held one is a FIGURE rather\n     than a picture somebody has to go and find: a row of counts in the gauge\n     face over a row of bars that grow from nothing the first time the section is\n     seen, with the tallest one lit. It is HTML — elements, grid and flex,\n     borders and backgrounds on the palette's tokens — because that is what a\n     figure is in this format, and it is what every page copied from here should\n     start from rather than a grey rectangle with a filename in it.\n\n     WHERE THE WORDS LIVE. All of them are in `content.yaml`. The argument and\n     the caption are markdown parts; the FIGURE's own words are the section's\n     `variables`, as parallel lists — `kpiName[1]` and `kpiValue[1]` are one\n     count, `barName[2]` and `barValue[2]` are one bar. So editing the document\n     edits the picture, and this file never has to be opened to change what the\n     drawing says.\n\n     WHAT TO CHANGE FIRST: those lists, and then the prose beside them. A number\n     per bar cannot live in a list SLOT — a slot holds markdown, and reading a\n     figure back out of prose is parsing — so the figures are variables, which\n     are edited on the page's own config screen rather than on the page. That is\n     the one thing here that does not work like the rest, and the caption says so\n     on the page rather than leaving a reader to find out.\n\n     THE PICTURE STAYS PUT WHILE THE ARGUMENT SCROLLS PAST IT. That works because\n     the page's one frame fills the canvas and scrolls inside itself, so the box\n     is its own viewport and `position: sticky` has a scroller to stick against.\n\n     ITS LIMIT IS THE SECTION, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS ONE SECTION AND NOT TWO.\n     Sticky only pins within its containing block, so the figure is released the\n     moment this section's box ends — it cannot stay held while the page scrolls\n     past the section below. A sequence choreographed across a scroll belongs in\n     ONE section for the same reason. What that costs is independent handles:\n     three sections would be three things a person can reorder and delete on\n     their own, and this is one. Choose per case and say which you chose.\n\n     ONE HOT COLOUR, SPENT ONCE. The tallest bar and its label take `--cyan` and\n     nothing else on the drawing is coloured at all, because a figure where\n     everything is lit has lit nothing. Which bar that is comes out of the\n     numbers rather than out of a class typed into a file.\n\n     IT ARRIVES, AND ONLY ONCE. `reveal` adds `is-seen` the first time the\n     section comes into view and disconnects; the bars grow and the counts rise\n     under that class, every duration multiplied by `--motion` — which the\n     document declares as 1 and as 0 under `prefers-reduced-motion`. The\n     unfinished state is written under `:not(.is-seen)`, so a reader who asked\n     for stillness gets the finished figure and no move at all, and so does a\n     bar added after the reveal has already fired.\n\n     THE COLLAPSE IS A CONTAINER QUERY, which asks how wide THIS SECTION is. A\n     media query would ask how wide the whole canvas is and get the wrong answer\n     wherever the section is not the full width of it. Sticky survives\n     `container-type` — checked in a browser rather than reasoned about, because\n     the box is a sandboxed frame and several ordinary behaviours differ in it. -->\n<style>\n  /* THE SECTION IS THE CONTAINER, and it has to say so: `@container` matches\n     nothing until an ancestor declares `container-type`. Nothing declares it for\n     you. */\n  :scope { display: block; container-type: inline-size; padding-block: 2.5rem; }\n\n  .spread {\n    display: grid;\n    gap: 1.5rem 2.5rem;\n    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr);\n    max-inline-size: 64rem;\n    margin-inline: auto;\n    padding-inline: 1.25rem;\n    align-items: start;\n    font-family: var(--sheet-face);\n    color: var(--ink);\n    line-height: 1.6;\n  }\n\n  /* A MEASURE ON THE PROSE, IN `rem`, so it tracks the reader's type size. The\n     sheet has no width of its own any more — this is the section's own choice,\n     and the band around it is free to go edge to edge. */\n  .say { max-inline-size: 34rem; }\n  .say > :first-child { margin-block-start: 0; }\n  .say > :last-child { margin-block-end: 0; }\n\n  /* THE HELD HALF. `top` is measured against the frame, which is the canvas the\n     host handed over — a viewport length in a section is an ordinary thing to\n     write here.\n\n     `margin: 0` BECAUSE THIS IS A `<figure>`, and the browser gives one 16px of\n     block margin and 40px on each side before anybody writes a rule. Measured:\n     in this two-column band the plate came out 261px wide inside a 341px track —\n     a quarter of the column gone to a margin nothing in this file asked for, and\n     nothing on screen to say where it went. */\n  .plate {\n    position: sticky;\n    top: 1.5rem;\n    margin: 0;\n    min-inline-size: 0;\n    display: grid;\n    gap: .5rem;\n  }\n\n  /* THE INSTRUMENT. A plate the drawing sits on, so the figure reads as one\n     object rather than as two rows that happen to be near each other. */\n  .panel {\n    display: grid;\n    gap: .9rem;\n    padding: .85rem 1rem 1rem;\n    border: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--stock-hi) 65%, transparent);\n  }\n\n  /* THE COUNTS. Auto-fit, so three become four by writing a fourth name and a\n     fourth figure and nothing here learns a number. */\n  .kpis {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(5.5rem, 100%), 1fr));\n    gap: .5rem .75rem;\n  }\n  .kpi { display: grid; gap: .1rem; padding-block-start: .4rem; border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule); }\n  .k {\n    font-family: var(--furniture-face);\n    font-size: .62rem;\n    letter-spacing: .1em;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    color: var(--ink-3);\n  }\n  /* THE GAUGE ROLE, WITH TABULAR FIGURES, because a column of numbers that\n     shifts as it counts is a column nobody can read down. */\n  .v {\n    font-family: var(--gauge-face);\n    font-size: 1.3rem;\n    line-height: 1.1;\n    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;\n  }\n\n  /* THE BARS. `flex: 1 1 0` on every bar and on every label, so the two rows\n     share one set of columns without either of them being told how many. */\n  .plot { display: grid; gap: .35rem; }\n  .bars {\n    display: flex;\n    align-items: flex-end;\n    gap: .3rem;\n    block-size: 7rem;\n    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);\n  }\n  .bar {\n    flex: 1 1 0;\n    min-inline-size: 0;\n    block-size: var(--h, 0%);\n    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 20%, transparent);\n    transform-origin: 50% 100%;\n    transition: transform calc(520ms * var(--motion, 1)) cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);\n    transition-delay: calc(var(--i, 0) * 70ms * var(--motion, 1));\n  }\n  /* THE ONE HOT COLOUR, ON THE ONE BAR THAT EARNED IT. */\n  .bar.is-lit { background: var(--cyan); }\n  .foot { display: flex; gap: .3rem; }\n  .foot > * {\n    flex: 1 1 0;\n    min-inline-size: 0;\n    text-align: center;\n    font-family: var(--gauge-face);\n    font-size: .68rem;\n    color: var(--ink-3);\n  }\n  /* `--cyan-t` AND NOT `--cyan` WHEREVER THE COLOUR IS TEXT: the `-t` pair is\n     the half of the accent it is safe to read words in, on a light palette and\n     on a dark one. */\n  .foot > .is-lit { color: var(--cyan-t); }\n\n  /* BEFORE IT IS SEEN: the plate, the counts' labels and the axis are already\n     there and only the figures are missing. The drawing is legible at every\n     frame of this, which is the bar — a reveal that starts from nothing is a\n     section that is blank while it plays. */\n  .bar { transform: scaleY(0); }\n  .v { opacity: 0; transition: opacity calc(360ms * var(--motion, 1)) ease calc(160ms * var(--motion, 1)); }\n\n  /* AND THE CLASS THE `reveal` PLUGIN ADDS. It is already on the section by the\n     time a bar added later is drawn, so a figure that grows after the reveal has\n     fired arrives finished rather than invisible. */\n  :scope.is-seen .bar { transform: none; }\n  :scope.is-seen .v { opacity: 1; }\n\n  /* A READER WHO ASKED FOR LESS MOTION GETS THE FIGURE AND NOT THE MOVE. The\n     durations are already nothing through `--motion`; this is the half a\n     multiplier cannot do, which is putting the ink and the height back. */\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n    .bar { transform: none; }\n    .v { opacity: 1; }\n  }\n\n  /* THE FIGURE'S WORDS, under it. Quieter and smaller than the argument, which\n     is the whole of what a caption has to be. */\n  .caption { font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink-3); }\n  .caption > :first-child { margin-block-start: 0; }\n  .caption > :last-child { margin-block-end: 0; }\n\n  /* THE FAULT IS A SLOT LIKE EVERY OTHER WORD HERE, hidden until the script says\n     otherwise. A blank rectangle where a drawing was is indistinguishable from a\n     page that is still loading, and the person looking at it is usually the\n     person who could have fixed it. */\n  .fault { display: none; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--ink-3); }\n  :scope.is-fault .fault { display: block; }\n  :scope.is-fault .panel { display: none; }\n\n  /* NARROW: ONE COLUMN, with the picture moved ABOVE the prose rather than left\n     at the bottom of it. In `rem`, so the breakpoint tracks the reader's type\n     size rather than a device's pixels.\n\n     IT HAS TO COME LAST. It overrides `.spread` and `.plate` above it, and at\n     equal specificity the later rule wins — declared earlier it would lose and\n     the collapse would silently never happen. That is the failure mode to watch\n     for in this file: an earlier version wrote `@media (max-inline-size: …)`,\n     which is not a media feature at all, so the query matched nothing and looked\n     exactly like a rule that worked. R60 fails that now. */\n  @container (width <= 52rem) {\n    .spread { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }\n    .plate { position: static; order: -1; }\n  }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"spread\">\n  <!-- Divs and not `<p>`s: a markdown slot is filled with rendered prose, and\n       rendered prose is paragraphs. -->\n  <div class=\"say\" data-g-part=\"body\"></div>\n  <figure class=\"plate\">\n    <div class=\"panel\"></div>\n    <div class=\"fault\" data-g-part=\"fault\"></div>\n    <figcaption class=\"caption\" data-g-part=\"caption\"></figcaption>\n  </figure>\n  <!-- ONE CLASS, ONCE, ON THIS SECTION, and the observer disconnects after it. -->\n  <span data-g-plugin=\"biom-reveal\"></span>\n</div>\n<script>\n  /* THE DRAWING, AND NOTHING ELSE. Every word in it comes out of `ctx.vars` —\n     the page's variables and the section's, merged with the nearest last — so\n     the figure is edited in `content.yaml` beside the prose that explains it. */\n\n  /** A variable that is a list, a lone scalar, or absent — all three arrive here\n   *  and leave as an array of strings. A one-item list is written as a scalar by\n   *  most people writing YAML, so reading only the list form would break the\n   *  smallest figure somebody draws. */\n  function list(value) {\n    if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(function (v) { return String(v).trim(); });\n    if (value === undefined || value === null || value === \"\") return [];\n    return [String(value).trim()];\n  }\n\n  /** One element, with its class, appended where it belongs. */\n  function add(parent, tag, cls) {\n    const el = document.createElement(tag);\n    if (cls) el.setAttribute(\"class\", cls);\n    parent.appendChild(el);\n    return el;\n  }\n\n  const vars = ctx.vars || {};\n  const kpiName = list(vars.kpiName);\n  const kpiValue = list(vars.kpiValue);\n  const barName = list(vars.barName);\n  const barValue = list(vars.barValue).map(Number);\n\n  const panel = section.querySelector(\".panel\");\n\n  /* NOTHING TO DRAW IS NOT AN EMPTY FIGURE, it is a section whose numbers have\n     not been written yet — so it says so, in its own slot. */\n  if (kpiName.length === 0 && barName.length === 0) {\n    section.classList.add(\"is-fault\");\n  } else {\n    /* THE DRAWING SAYS WHAT IT IS, to a reader who is not looking at it. The\n       words are the document's own, so there is nothing here to keep in step. */\n    panel.setAttribute(\"role\", \"img\");\n    panel.setAttribute(\"aria-label\", kpiName.concat(barName).join(\", \"));\n\n    if (kpiName.length > 0) {\n      const kpis = add(panel, \"div\", \"kpis\");\n      kpiName.forEach(function (name, i) {\n        const cell = add(kpis, \"div\", \"kpi\");\n        add(cell, \"div\", \"k\").textContent = name;\n        /* A COUNT WITH NO FIGURE BESIDE IT IS STILL A COUNT — the lists are\n           parallel and a short one is somebody mid-edit, not a broken page. */\n        add(cell, \"div\", \"v\").textContent = kpiValue[i] === undefined ? \"\" : kpiValue[i];\n      });\n    }\n\n    /* THE TALLEST BAR IS FOUND RATHER THAN MARKED, so the lit one moves when the\n       numbers do and there is no class in a file to keep in step with them. */\n    const usable = barValue.filter(function (v) { return Number.isFinite(v); });\n    const top = usable.length > 0 ? Math.max.apply(null, usable) : 0;\n    const best = barValue.indexOf(top);\n\n    if (barName.length > 0) {\n      const plot = add(panel, \"div\", \"plot\");\n      const bars = add(plot, \"div\", \"bars\");\n      const foot = add(plot, \"div\", \"foot\");\n      barName.forEach(function (name, i) {\n        const value = Number.isFinite(barValue[i]) ? barValue[i] : 0;\n        const lit = i === best && top > 0;\n        const bar = add(bars, \"div\", lit ? \"bar is-lit\" : \"bar\");\n        /* THE ONE THING A SCRIPT MAY WRITE ONTO AN ELEMENT'S STYLE IS A CUSTOM\n           PROPERTY. The stylesheet decides what the height and the stagger do\n           with it, and the palette still decides what anything resolves to. */\n        bar.style.setProperty(\"--h\", (top > 0 ? Math.max(2, Math.round((value / top) * 100)) : 2) + \"%\");\n        bar.style.setProperty(\"--i\", String(i));\n        bar.title = name + \": \" + (Number.isFinite(barValue[i]) ? barValue[i] : \"\");\n        add(foot, \"span\", lit ? \"is-lit\" : \"\").textContent = name;\n      });\n    }\n  }\n</script>\n",
      "fallback": false,
      "parts": {
        "body": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "md": "## What the section can do that the document could not\n\nA section is a div. It is full width, it owns its layout entirely, and the reading measure\nlives inside the default section's own file rather than on the sheet — so a band that goes\nedge to edge costs nothing, and a column that wants 34rem of measure asks for it here.\n\n> Prose still wants a measure. A page that is edge to edge everywhere is exactly as\n> monotonous as one that is 34rem everywhere.\n\nThe freedom is to choose per section. Two columns where a figure earns its place beside the\nargument; one column where the words are the whole of it; a grid where things are being\ncompared. A page that chooses the same shape every time has not used any of it.\n\nSwap this slot's prose for the real argument, and swap `kpiName`, `kpiValue`, `barName` and\n`barValue` for the real figures. The tallest bar lights itself, so nothing here has to be\ntold which one matters.\n",
          "vars": {
